Local material extraction reduces transportation impacts and strengthens regional economic resilience while creating opportunities for circular material flows at the community level. By designing products to utilize locally-sourced materials, companies can reduce embodied carbon associated with supply chain logistics while supporting local suppliers and traditional craft knowledge. This approach requires designers to deeply understand regional material availability, properties, and cultural significance. From a circular perspective, locally-extracted materials often have established reverse logistics networks and cultural understanding around reuse, making end-of-life management more straightforward.